{
 "language": "bn",
 "title": "Bengali grammar",
 "intro": "A working reference for beginners to intermediate learners, in standard colloquial Bengali (চলিত ভাষা) as spoken in Bangladesh and West Bengal. Bengali has no grammatical gender, puts the verb at the end and attaches endings to nouns: read a section, notice the shape, then listen for it in what you watch and read.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "number": 1,
   "id": "word-order",
   "title": "Word order & \"to be\"",
   "kind": "structure",
   "level": "A1",
   "tags": [
    "word order",
    "SOV",
    "verb last",
    "to be",
    "zero copula",
    "কি"
   ],
   "rule": null,
   "text": "The verb comes last: **আমি ভাত খাই** (I rice eat). In the present, \"is/am/are\" is usually left out: **আমি ছাত্র** (I [am a] student). A yes/no question adds **কি** after the subject or simply rises in tone.",
   "tables": [],
   "after": null,
   "examples": [
    {
     "text": "আমি প্রতিদিন বাংলা পড়ি।",
     "translation": "I study Bengali every day.",
     "note": null,
     "reading": null
    },
    {
     "text": "ও আমার বন্ধু।",
     "translation": "He/she is my friend.",
     "note": "no verb for 'is'",
     "reading": null
    },
    {
     "text": "আপনি কি চা খাবেন?",
     "translation": "Will you have some tea?",
     "note": "কি makes a yes/no question",
     "reading": null
    }
   ],
   "points": [],
   "tip": null
  },
  {
   "number": 2,
   "id": "pronouns",
   "title": "Pronouns & the three levels of \"you\"",
   "kind": "structure",
   "level": "A1",
   "tags": [
